Verification rules:
- Preserve valid tables and their source order.
- Check the full page, including small tables and tables beginning at page top.
- Recheck every row classification from its cells. If a line has a value in
  any detected data column, include it in rows as a data row even when its
  first-column label is bold, names a subgroup, or also introduces following
  rows. Only a line with no data-column values may be described as a
  section-only row.
- Preserve a structured index or list with aligned labels and a value or page
  column as a table even when it has no drawn grid.
- Evaluate continuation independently of header_present. A continuation may
  repeat its column headers.
- Repeated column headers do not establish a new logical table. A later-page
  indicator, table footer, or rows continuing without a new table title or
  section is continuation evidence even when header_present is true.
- Before returning, ignore the previous continuation decision and perform a
  mandatory continuation audit for every detected table. When a
  current-of-total marker belongs to the table, it governs the corrected
  decision:
  1. Read and quote the exact marker in continuation_rationale.
  2. If the current number is 1, set is_continuation to false. Do not confuse
     the physical document page number with the marker's current number.
  3. If the current number is greater than 1, set is_continuation to true even
     when column headers repeat. This is direct transcription of the marker,
     not an inference from the page contents.
  4. This marker decision is authoritative for that table. A title, section, or
     repeated header on the current page does not override it.
  5. A section or subgroup label inside the table's first column is not a table
     title. A different table title must name the whole table outside its column
     headers and data rows.
  6. When a physical page label and a separate current-of-total marker both
     appear, use the separate marker for the table sequence.
  7. After writing continuation_rationale, verify that is_continuation matches
     the conclusion stated in the rationale.
- Mark a table as a continuation when the page begins inside an existing
  logical table and no new table title or section establishes a new table.
- Do not mark continuation from column similarity alone.
- Use table_classification "OTHER"; downstream extraction uses the supplied
  schema rather than a domain classification.
- Every line_number must exist in the prepared page text. Never invent columns
  or tables.

